<meta name="socialpost1" content="Dr. Craig Towers was hailed as a hero for his research on detoxing pregnant opioid users. After he published a study that found naltrexone could help patients stay off drugs, addiction specialists started asking if his work was too good to be true." />
<meta name="socialpost2" content="Medical doctrine has long held that pregnant, opioid-addicted patients could not be safely detoxed. Dr. Craig Towers published a study that found otherwise. Then experts began to raise concerns." />
<meta name="socialpost3" content="Dr. Craig Towers brought over 25 years of experience to his role as an OB-GYN in Tennessee. But when he claimed in a study that naltrexone could help people stay sober, addiction recovery specialists began to question whether the research held up." />
<meta name="socialpost4" content="In media coverage, OB-GYN Dr. Craig Towers came across as gently protective of his patients. But one patient said she felt pressured to take naltrexone, a drug Towers was researching to see if it could keep pregnant patients off opiates." />
<meta name="socialpost5" content="When a doctor published research claiming naltrexone could help pregnant women stay sober, experts sounded the alarm. “I am concerned that each day the article(s) is out there, potential harm to a vulnerable population accumulates,” one wrote." />
